desc: PolyTrills - Polyphonic MIDI Trill Generator
// @description PolyTrills
// @author Giorgio Robino
// @version 0.1.0
// @about
//   Turns the notes you hold into trills. One key passes through, two alternate,
//   three give a sustained drone plus a trill, four give two parallel trills --
//   each voice on the MIDI channel it was played on.
//
//   Rate is in notes per second and does not follow the tempo: a trill is a
//   gesture, not a metrical value. Humanize makes the alternation breathe,
//   Balance gives one of the two notes a little more time, and Ramp Up / Ramp
//   Down let the trill lean in and die away.
//
//   The defaults are not a taste: they come from measuring recorded piano
//   performances -- about 12 notes a second, an alternation that is uneven by
//   roughly 20%, and every note ending slightly *before* the next attack instead
//   of on top of it. The corpus, the instrument that measures it, the method and
//   the reference values are published in corpus/.
// @changelog
//   0.1.0 -- First release. Rate in notes per second, Humanize, Balance, Ramp
//   Up / Ramp Down / Ramp Depth, drone and two parallel trills, transport-stop
//   flush.
//

// ============================================================
// PolyTrills  —  JSFX MIDI Plugin for REAPER
// ============================================================

// ── MUSICIAN'S GUIDE ────────────────────────────────────────
//
//  PolyTrills intercepts your MIDI notes in real-time and turns
//  them into expressive trills — rapid alternations between
//  two pitches — without you having to play them manually.
//  Just hold the notes that define your trill and the plugin
//  does the work, at the rate you set — a trill is a gesture,
//  not a metrical value, so the tempo is not consulted.
//
//  WHAT HAPPENS DEPENDING ON HOW MANY KEYS YOU HOLD:
//
//   ┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
//   │ 1 NOTE  — Pass-through. No trill. The note sounds    │
//   │           exactly as played. PolyTrills is transparent.   │
//   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
//   │ 2 NOTES — Classic trill. The lower note is the BASE, │
//   │           the higher note is the UPPER. PolyTrills         │
//   │           alternates between them at the set rate.   │
//   │           Any interval works: semitone, tone, third, │
//   │           fourth, fifth — you decide by what you      │
//   │           play. Note assignment is always by pitch    │
//   │           (lowest = base), never by press order.     │
//   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
//   │ 3 NOTES — Drone + Trill. The LOWEST note becomes a   │
//   │           sustained drone (pedal point). The MIDDLE  │
//   │           and HIGHEST notes form the trill pair.     │
//   │           Great for organ-like pedal-point effects.  │
//   ├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
//   │ 4 NOTES — Two parallel trills, running simultaneously │
//   │           at the same rate:                          │
//   │             Trill A: note 1 (lowest) ↔ note 2        │
//   │             Trill B: note 3 ↔ note 4 (highest)       │
//   │           Think two orchestral parts each trilling.  │
//   └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
//
//  PolyTrills FEEL & EXPRESSION:
//   - Rate is how many notes per second the trill emits: a
//     trill is a gesture, so it does not follow the tempo. An
//     ordinary piano trill is about 12 notes a second.
//   - Humanize is what makes it human, and it does three
//     things: each note has its own length (some hesitate),
//     the rate wanders slowly, and the first notes start a
//     little slower and softer. At 0 you get a metronome.
//   - Ramp Up and Ramp Down make the trill lean in and die away:
//     the alternation starts (or ends) sparse and settles, over a
//     time you set in seconds. Ramp Depth says how sparse.
//   - Each note also *ends* a little before the next attack,
//     which is how a player articulates a trill: the notes
//     stay separate instead of smearing into each other.
//   - Balance gives one of the two notes a little more time
//     than the other (±15%): a trill is not an arpeggio.
//   - When you lift all fingers, the trill keeps running for
//     the Linger window and then stops cleanly. Linger 0 stops
//     it at the note-off, which is what an ending "on the beat"
//     needs.
//   - Every generated note keeps the MIDI channel of the note that
//     created it: a trill played on channel 3 stays on channel 3.
//
// ── DEVELOPER / LLM NOTES ───────────────────────────────────
//
//  ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW (r10):
//   One trill schedule drives one or two voices. The schedule owns
//   the timing (tr_on_in, tr_off_in, tr_period, tr_drift, tr_count);
//   a voice owns only its pitches and channel (t1_*, t2_*). Both
//   voices are emitted inside the same offset, so 4-note mode cannot
//   drift apart and the stream cannot go backwards.
//
//   The held note list (held_notes[], held_vels[],
//   held_channels[]) is always kept sorted ascending by pitch, so
//   note-assignment logic is deterministic and order-independent.
//   A held note is identified by its (pitch, channel) pair: the same
//   pitch on two channels counts as two notes.
//
//   next_period() returns the samples between two attacks: the
//   nominal Rate, the onset lean-in, a log-normal jitter and a slow
//   drift. It is called once per note, so there is no "cycle" and no
//   half period any more. The note itself sounds for GATE of that
//   period and is released by a scheduled event, which is why a trill
//   is slightly detached. Tempo is not read: the rate is the player's,
//   not the metronome's.
//
//   In @block:
//    1. Incoming MIDI Note-On/Off events update held_notes[]
//       and, only when the held set really changed, trigger
//       reconfigure(), which stops all engines and restarts them
//       for the new note configuration.
//    2. How far the engines may advance is decided: the whole
//       block, or only up to the sample where the tail expires.
//    3. advance_trill() walks the schedule through that range,
//       merging the pending release with the next attack so that
//       midisend() offsets only ever increase.
//    4. A tail countdown silences the trill after all notes lift; if
//       Ramp Down is set, the rate decelerates across the tail (r12).
//
//   EVENT OFFSET INVARIANT (r1): every midisend() in a block uses an
//   offset inside 0 … samplesblock-1, and offsets never go backwards.
//   The schedule therefore carries tr_anchor, the block offset of the
//   attack that set its countdowns; advance_trill() begins the
//   timeline there and resets it to 0. Every midisend() also records
//   last_ofs, and the final note-offs of the tail are fenced at
//   max(expiry, last_ofs) so they can never precede an event of the
//   same block — the case that matters when Linger is 0.
//
//  MIDI PASSTHROUGH:
//   All non-note MIDI (CC, pitch bend, aftertouch, program
//   change, etc.) is passed through unmodified.
//
//  EXTENDING THIS PLUGIN:
//   - 5+ notes: could add a 3rd trill engine or implement
//     cycling through all pitches (roulade mode).
//   - Mordent/Turn: detect rapid note-on/off sequences to
//     trigger ornament shapes instead of sustained trills.
//   - Per-trill balance: split the Balance slider into two,
//     one for each trill engine in 4-note mode.
//   - Humanization: add slight random jitter to note offsets.
//
// ============================================================


// ── SLIDERS ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Six controls. Three describe the trill itself (Rate, Humanize, Balance),
// three the shape of its ends (Ramp Up, Ramp Down, Ramp Depth). There is no
// channel control: every note keeps the channel it was played on (r13).

// Rate: notes per second, attack to attack. A trill is a physical
// gesture: the pianist's rate does not follow the metronome, so this is
// an absolute speed and the tempo is not read at all (r8).
//   4  → slow, deliberate; the interval is heard as two separate notes
//   8  → about a 16th at 120 BPM: the old ripple, for comparison
//   12 → an ordinary trill: the median of the reference measurements
//        (see corpus/README.md) [default]
//   16 → fast. Many sampled pianos cannot retrigger this fast: the plugin
//        asks, the library answers.
//   20 → virtuoso; above this it stops sounding like an ornament.
slider1:12<4,20,0.5>Rate (notes/s)

// Humanize: how human the alternation sounds. One control for three
// things a machine does not do (r10):
//   - the length of each note is jittered with a log-normal factor, so
//     most notes are near the nominal length and a few hesitate; the
//     coefficient of variation is ~0.21 at 70, which is what the corpus
//     measured on real performances (0.20), and ~0.30 at 100;
//   - a slow drift wanders over ~10 notes, because no trill holds one
//     rate for long;
//   - the first ~4 notes start slightly slower and softer.
//   0   → machine-even, the reference the offline tests use
//   70  → the corpus median [default]
//   100 → looser than most performers
slider2:70<0,100,1>Humanize (%)

// Balance: time split between the two notes of the pair.
//   0   → the base note is 15% longer, the upper a quick grace note
//   50  → even [default]
//   100 → the upper is 15% longer
// ±15% is the spread the corpus measured (upper/base 0.74 … 1.37), not a
// dramatic gesture: a trill is not an arpeggio.
slider3:50<0,100,1>Balance

// Ramp Up: how long the trill takes to reach its final rate. At the first
// attack it runs at 1/(1+Ramp Depth) of the Rate and converges linearly,
// note by note, over this time (r12).
//   0   → no ramp: the trill starts already at speed [default]
//   2   → a trill that settles in two seconds
// This is an expressive control, not a realism one: the corpus says a real
// trill starts only 1.11x slower, so 0 is the measured behaviour.
slider4:0<0,10,0.05>Ramp Up (s)

// Ramp Down: how long the trill takes to die away after you lift ALL keys.
// The end is not knowable in advance — the plugin learns it when the last
// key is released — so this window *extends* the sound: the trill keeps
// alternating and slows down from the final rate to 1/(1+Ramp Depth) of it,
// then stops. It replaces the old Linger, which was the same tail at a
// constant rate (0.1 s here reproduces Linger 100 ms).
//   0    → stop at the last played event (an ending on the beat)
//   0.1  → the short throw-away of a played trill [default]
//   3    → the trill dissolves over three seconds
slider5:0.1<0,10,0.05>Ramp Down (s)

// Ramp Depth: how slow the trill is at the two ends of the ramp.
//   0   → no ramp effect at all, even with Ramp Up/Down set
//   60  → the trill starts and ends at 1/2.8 of the Rate [default]
//   100 → it starts and ends at 1/4 of the Rate
// The same depth at both ends: one number, two ramps.
slider6:60<0,100,1>Ramp Depth (%)




// ── INIT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
@init

// ── Memory layout ──
// JSFX global memory is accessed as base_pointer[index].
// We allocate three parallel arrays of 4 slots each:
//   Slots  0– 3 : held note MIDI pitch values (0–127)
//   Slots  4– 7 : held note MIDI velocities   (1–127)
//   Slots  8–11 : held note MIDI channels     (0–15)
// A slot is identified by (pitch, channel); the pitch array is kept
// sorted ascending, ties broken by channel.
held_notes    = 0;
held_vels     = 4;
held_channels = 8;
// Next free slot: 12 (extend here if more state arrays are needed)

// Number of notes currently held by the player (0–4).
held_count = 0;

// ── Trill schedule (r10) ──
// One schedule drives up to two trill voices. The two parallel trills of
// 4-note mode are one rhythm, so they share every period and are emitted
// at the same offsets: ordering is then a property of the structure, not
// something to sort afterwards (r6).
//
// A note is no longer "half of a cycle": it has its own attack-to-attack
// period, its own sounding length (GATE of the period) and its own random
// jitter. The release is therefore scheduled *after* the attack that
// created it and may fall in a later block: tr_off_in is that pending
// note-off, and a measured trill releases each note ~25 ms before the
// next attack (gate 0.72) instead of holding it for the whole interval.
//
// The two countdowns are samples from the current walk position. At the
// end of a block they are converted to "from the block start", which is
// what carries them across the boundary without any absolute time.
tr_active = 0;         // 1 = a trill is running
tr_count  = 0;         // notes played since it started (onset shaping)
tr_on_in  = 1000000;   // samples until the next attack
tr_off_in = 1000000;   // samples until the pending release
tr_period = 6000;      // samples of the last attack-to-attack interval
tr_drift  = 0;         // slow wandering of the rate, -0.25 … 0.25
tr_anchor = 0;         // block offset where the countdowns were set (r1)

// ── Trill voice 1 ──
// Active for 2-note (pair), 3-note (middle↔high) and 4-note (lowest pair).
t1_active   = 0;
t1_phase    = 0;    // which pitch the *next* attack plays (0 base, 1 upper)
t1_sounding = -1;   // pitch currently ON, -1 = none
t1_base     = -1;
t1_upper    = -1;
t1_ch       = 0;

// ── Trill voice 2 ──
// 4-note mode only (3rd↔4th held note).
t2_active   = 0;
t2_phase    = 0;
t2_sounding = -1;
t2_base     = -1;
t2_upper    = -1;
t2_ch       = 0;

// ── Drone state (3-note mode only) ──
// The lowest held note is passed through as a sustained note.
drone_active = 0;
drone_note   = -1;
drone_ch     = 0;

// ── Pass-through state (1-note mode) ──
// The single held note is forwarded as-is.
pass_note = -1;
pass_ch   = 0;

// ── Tail state (r12) ──
// The tail is what used to be the linger window: after the last key is
// released the trill keeps running for a while and then stops. r12 gives
// that window a shape — the rate decelerates across it — so the tail is
// also the ramp-down, and "Ramp Down 0" is the old "Linger 0".
tail_active  = 0;
tail_counter = 0;   // samples remaining in the tail
tail_total   = 0;   // samples of the whole tail (to place the ramp)

// ── Ramp state (r12) ──
tr_elapsed  = 0;    // samples since the first attack of this trill
ramp_up_s   = 0;    // samples of the ramp-up window (from slider4)
ramp_down_s = 0;    // samples of the tail (from slider5)

// ── Transport watch (r4) ──
// play_state is 0=stopped, <0=error, 1=playing, 2=paused,
// 5=recording, 6=record paused (JSFX reference, special variables).
TRANS_UNSEEN     = -99;
trans_prev_state = TRANS_UNSEEN;
trans_adv_prev   = 0;
trans_adv_now    = 0;
trans_parked     = 0;

// ── Per-block scratch ──
advance_len    = 0;
fence_ofs      = 0;
tail_expired   = 0;
last_ofs       = 0;

// ── Constants of the gesture model ──
// All measured on the corpus in corpus/README.md, not invented.
GATE         = 0.72;   // a note sounds for 72% of its period: the measured
                       // release is ~25 ms before the next attack
ONSET_NOTES  = 4;      // the first notes are slower and softer
ONSET_SLOW   = 0.11;   // measured: first interval 1.11x the body
ONSET_SOFT   = 0.60;   // first note at 60% of the target velocity
HUMAN_SIGMA  = 0.30;   // log-normal spread at Humanize 100 (CV ~0.30)
DRIFT_STEP   = 0.03;   // random-walk step of the slow rate drift
DRIFT_KEEP   = 0.92;   // how long the drift remembers (about 12 notes)
BALANCE_SPAN = 0.15;   // Balance ±15% (corpus: upper/base 0.74 … 1.37)
RAMP_MAX     = 3.0;    // Ramp Depth 100 → the ends run at 1/4 of the Rate
MIN_NOTE     = 200;    // samples: floor of one note period (well above one
                       // audio block, so a block never chokes on toggles)

// master_vel: the velocity carried into each generated note. Set to the
// average velocity of the relevant held notes by reconfigure().
master_vel = 80;

// ── FUNCTIONS ───────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// Each function is defined before the section code that calls it
// (@init below, then @slider and @block), because JSFX compiles the
// file in textual order. They sit here, in the @init region, rather
// than above @init: that is the layout used by every stock and
// third-party JSFX measured on this machine (199 of 199 files that
// define functions at all), and without a compiler outside REAPER a
// deviation from it cannot be tested.
//
// Function bodies do not run at definition time; the globals they
// read are initialised in the @init block above.

// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: transport_advancing(state)
//
// True when the transport is moving forward: 1 = playing,
// 5 = recording. 0 = stopped, 2 = paused, 6 = record paused, and
// anything negative is the documented error state.
//
//  state   : a play_state value, or TRANS_UNSEEN
//  returns : 1 or 0
// ============================================================
function transport_advancing(state) (
  state > 0 && (state & 1);
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: human_factor()
//
// The multiplier applied to one note's period to make it human. It is
// log-normal, not uniform: real trills have a tight core of notes near
// the nominal length and a tail of hesitations, and a uniform jitter
// reproduces the spread without the shape (r10).
//
//   sigma = Humanize/100 * HUMAN_SIGMA   →   CV = sqrt(exp(sigma^2) - 1)
//     Humanize  70 → sigma 0.21 → CV 0.21   (corpus median: 0.20)
//     Humanize 100 → sigma 0.30 → CV 0.30
//     Humanize   0 → exactly 1.0, the metronome the offline tests use
//
// The normal deviate is the sum of four uniforms, standardised (mean 2,
// variance 1/3): cheap, bounded, close enough to normal. The result is
// clamped to 0.4 … 2.5 so a wild draw can never stall the trill.
// ============================================================
function human_factor()
  local(sigma, z)
(
  slider2 <= 0 ? (
    1.0;
  ) : (
    sigma = slider2 / 100.0 * HUMAN_SIGMA;
    z = (rand() + rand() + rand() + rand() - 2.0) * sqrt(3.0);
    // The -sigma^2/2 term divides out the log-normal's own mean, so the
    // *average* note length is the nominal one and Rate stays honest
    // instead of drifting ~2% slower as the jitter grows.
    min(2.5, max(0.4, exp(sigma * z - 0.5 * sigma * sigma)));
  );
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: onset_slow()
//
// The first interval of a real trill is 1.11x the body (measured), and
// the effect decays over about four notes. Scaled by Humanize, because
// it is a human trait: Humanize 0 gives a metronome from the first note.
// ============================================================
function onset_slow()
(
  tr_count >= ONSET_NOTES ? (
    1.0;
  ) : (
    1.0 + ONSET_SLOW * (slider2 / 100.0) *
          (ONSET_NOTES - tr_count) / ONSET_NOTES;
  );
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: onset_velocity(vel)
//
// The first notes are also softer: the recorded take shows about 60% of
// the target velocity on the first note, reaching it by the fourth. The
// depth is fixed and tied to Humanize, for the same reason as the rate
// onset.
// ============================================================
function onset_velocity(vel)
(
  tr_count >= ONSET_NOTES ? (
    max(1, min(127, floor(vel)));
  ) : (
    max(1, min(127, floor(vel * (ONSET_SOFT +
        (1.0 - ONSET_SOFT) * tr_count / ONSET_NOTES))));
  );
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: ramp_up_factor()
//
// How much longer than nominal the period is, this early in the trill.
// Ramp Up is a *time*, so the factor is driven by the elapsed samples:
// at the first attack it is 1+RAMP_MAX*depth (a slow, sparse alternation)
// and it converges linearly to 1 at the end of the window (r12).
// ============================================================
function ramp_up_factor()
(
  ramp_up_s <= 0 ? (
    1.0;
  ) : (
    1.0 + RAMP_MAX * (slider6 / 100.0) *
          max(0.0, 1.0 - tr_elapsed / ramp_up_s);
  );
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: ramp_down_factor()
//
// The mirror image, for the tail. The end of a trill cannot be known in
// advance, so the ramp-down runs inside the tail, whose total length is
// known the moment it starts: the period grows linearly from nominal to
// 1+RAMP_MAX*depth across it, and the trill dies away (r12).
// ============================================================
function ramp_down_factor()
(
  tail_active && tail_total > 0 ? (
    1.0 + RAMP_MAX * (slider6 / 100.0) * (1.0 - tail_counter / tail_total);
  ) : (
    1.0;
  );
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: next_period()
//
// Samples between two attacks: the nominal Rate, slowed by the onset for
// the first notes, multiplied by the human jitter, moved by the drift.
// Called once per generated note, so every note has its own length (r10)
// — there is no "cycle" any more.
//
// The drift is a random walk with a short memory: it wanders over about
// a dozen notes, which is what a trill that sags and recovers sounds
// like, and it mean-reverts, so it cannot accumulate.
// ============================================================
function next_period()
  local(pp)
(
  tr_drift = tr_drift * DRIFT_KEEP +
             (rand() * 2.0 - 1.0) * DRIFT_STEP * (slider2 / 100.0);
  tr_drift = max(-0.25, min(0.25, tr_drift));

  pp  = srate / max(0.5, slider1);      // nominal: notes/s → samples
  pp *= 1.0 + tr_drift;
  pp *= onset_slow();
  pp *= human_factor();
  pp *= ramp_up_factor();               // slow start, if asked (r12)
  pp *= ramp_down_factor();             // dying away, in the tail (r12)
  max(MIN_NOTE, floor(pp));
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: note_on(ofs, ch, pitch, vel)
// FUNCTION: note_off(ofs, ch, pitch)
//
// Thin wrappers around midisend() for readability.
// ofs   : sample offset within the current @block (0 … samplesblock-1)
// ch    : MIDI channel (0–15)
// pitch : MIDI note number (0–127)
// vel   : MIDI velocity (1–127); note_off sends 0
//
// Both also record last_ofs, the offset of the most recent event of the
// block: the final note-offs may never be sent before it.
// ============================================================
function note_on(ofs, ch, pitch, vel) (
  last_ofs = ofs;
  midisend(ofs, 0x90 | ch, pitch, vel);
);

function note_off(ofs, ch, pitch) (
  last_ofs = ofs;
  midisend(ofs, 0x80 | ch, pitch, 0);
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: insert_held(pitch, vel, ch)
//
// Inserts a new note into the held_notes[] array, keeping the
// array sorted in ascending pitch order (lowest first), ties on
// the same pitch ordered by channel.
//
// The identity of a held note is (pitch, channel), not pitch alone:
// two notes of the same pitch on different channels are legal in
// MIDI and are tracked as two separate entries. A (pitch, channel)
// pair already held, or a fifth note, is ignored.
//
//  pitch : MIDI note number to add
//  vel   : MIDI velocity of the note-on
//  ch    : MIDI channel of the note-on
//
//  Returns 1 if the held set changed, 0 if the note was ignored
//  (duplicate, or four notes already held). Callers use this to
//  avoid a needless reconfigure().
// ============================================================
function insert_held(pitch, vel, ch)
  local(i, j, added)
(
  added = 0;

  // Look for the same (pitch, channel) pair already in the list
  i = 0;
  while (i < held_count &&
         (held_notes[i] != pitch || held_channels[i] != ch)) ( i += 1; );

  // Only proceed if not already held and there is room
  i == held_count && held_count < 4 ? (

    // Find the sorted insertion index: after every entry with a lower
    // pitch, or the same pitch on a lower channel
    i = 0;
    while (i < held_count &&
           (held_notes[i] < pitch ||
            (held_notes[i] == pitch && held_channels[i] < ch))) ( i += 1; );

    // Shift existing entries one slot to the right to make room
    j = held_count;
    while (j > i) (
      held_notes[j]    = held_notes[j - 1];
      held_vels[j]     = held_vels[j - 1];
      held_channels[j] = held_channels[j - 1];
      j -= 1;
    );

    // Write new entry at the insertion point
    held_notes[i]    = pitch;
    held_vels[i]     = vel;
    held_channels[i] = ch;
    held_count += 1;
    added = 1;
  );

  added;
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: remove_held(pitch, ch)
//
// Removes the (pitch, channel) entry from the held_notes[] array.
// Shifts remaining entries left to close the gap, preserving the
// sorted order. A note-off on a different channel than the one held
// does not remove anything.
//
//  pitch : MIDI note number to remove
//  ch    : MIDI channel of the note-off
//
//  Returns 1 if the held set changed, 0 if no matching entry was
//  held (duplicate note-off, or note-off for a note PolyTrills dropped
//  because four were already held).
// ============================================================
function remove_held(pitch, ch)
  local(i, j, removed)
(
  removed = 0;

  // Locate the (pitch, channel) pair
  i = 0;
  while (i < held_count &&
         (held_notes[i] != pitch || held_channels[i] != ch)) ( i += 1; );

  i < held_count ? ( // found: remove it
    j = i;
    while (j < held_count - 1) (
      held_notes[j]    = held_notes[j + 1];
      held_vels[j]     = held_vels[j + 1];
      held_channels[j] = held_channels[j + 1];
      j += 1;
    );
    held_count -= 1;
    removed = 1;
  );

  removed;
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: stop_trill(ofs)
//
// Silences every trill voice and cancels the pending release. Safe when
// nothing is running. One call handles both voices, because they share
// one schedule (r10).
// ============================================================
function stop_trill(ofs) (
  t1_sounding >= 0 ? note_off(ofs, t1_ch, t1_sounding);
  t2_sounding >= 0 ? note_off(ofs, t2_ch, t2_sounding);
  t1_sounding = -1;
  t2_sounding = -1;
  t1_active   = 0;
  t2_active   = 0;
  tr_active   = 0;
  tr_on_in    = 1000000;
  tr_off_in   = 1000000;
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: stop_drone(ofs)
//
// Sends note-off for the active drone note (3-note mode).
// Safe to call when no drone is active.
// ============================================================
function stop_drone(ofs) (
  drone_active && drone_note >= 0 ? (
    note_off(ofs, drone_ch, drone_note);
    drone_note   = -1;
    drone_active = 0;
  );
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: stop_pass(ofs)
//
// Sends note-off for the active pass-through note (1-note mode).
// Safe to call when no pass-through note is active.
// ============================================================
function stop_pass(ofs) (
  pass_note >= 0 ? (
    note_off(ofs, pass_ch, pass_note);
    pass_note = -1;
  );
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: stop_all(ofs)
//
// Silences every voice: the trill (both voices and its pending release),
// the drone and the pass-through. Called at the start of every
// reconfigure() and when the tail expires.
// ============================================================
function stop_all(ofs) (
  stop_trill(ofs);
  stop_drone(ofs);
  stop_pass(ofs);
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: start_trill(b1, u1, ch1, b2, u2, ch2, voices, vel, ofs)
//
// Starts the trill with one or two voices and fires the first attack.
// Pitch state lives per voice; timing lives in the schedule, so the two
// voices of 4-note mode cannot drift apart.
//
//  b1, u1, ch1 : base, upper and MIDI channel of voice 1
//  b2, u2, ch2 : the same for voice 2, ignored when voices == 1
//  voices      : 1 or 2
//  vel         : target velocity (master_vel); the onset scales it
//  ofs         : block offset of the first attack
// ============================================================
function start_trill(b1, u1, ch1, b2, u2, ch2, voices, vel, ofs) (
  stop_trill(ofs);          // safety: silence anything residual

  t1_active = 1;
  t1_base   = b1;
  t1_upper  = u1;
  t1_ch     = ch1;
  t1_phase  = 0;
  t2_active = voices > 1;
  t2_base   = b2;
  t2_upper  = u2;
  t2_ch     = ch2;
  t2_phase  = 0;

  tr_active  = 1;
  tr_count   = 0;
  tr_drift   = 0;
  tr_elapsed = 0;

  // ── first attack ──
  // The phase is flipped *after* firing, so the next attack plays the other
  // note: without this the second note would repeat the first.
  t1_sounding = b1;
  note_on(ofs, t1_ch, t1_sounding, onset_velocity(vel));
  t1_phase = 1;
  t2_active ? (
    t2_sounding = b2;
    note_on(ofs, t2_ch, t2_sounding, onset_velocity(vel));
    t2_phase = 1;
  );
  tr_count += 1;

  tr_period = next_period();
  tr_off_in = max(64, floor(tr_period * GATE));
  tr_on_in  = tr_period;
  tr_anchor = ofs;
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: hold_note(pitch, vel, ch, ofs)
//
// Sustains one note without trilling it. Used when a trill pair
// would have equal pitches (r7): one channel, one pitch, so
// alternating would retrigger instead of trill.
//
// The voice is the first free sustained one: the pass-through voice,
// else the drone (free in 2- and 4-note modes, taken by the lowest
// note in 3-note mode). At most two such pairs exist, so a third call
// cannot happen; if it ever did, the drone's sounding note is
// note-off'd before being reused, so no voice is left on.
//
//  pitch : MIDI pitch to sustain
//  vel   : velocity for the note-on
//  ch    : MIDI channel for the note-on
//  ofs   : sample offset for the note-on
// ============================================================
function hold_note(pitch, vel, ch, ofs) (
  pass_note < 0 ? (
    pass_note = pitch;
    pass_ch   = ch;
  ) : (
    drone_active && drone_note >= 0 ? note_off(ofs, drone_ch, drone_note);
    drone_note   = pitch;
    drone_ch     = ch;
    drone_active = 1;
  );
  note_on(ofs, ch, pitch, vel);
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: reconfigure(ofs)
//
// The central state machine. Called whenever the set of held
// notes changes (note-on or note-off while notes remain held).
//
// Steps:
//  1. Stop all currently active voices.
//  2. Compute master_vel from held note velocities.
//  3. Recompute rhythmic periods (calc_half_periods).
//  4. Start the correct engines/drone/pass-through for the
//     new held_count.
//
//  ofs : sample offset within the current @block for all
//        note events generated by this call.
//
//  Note: never called when held_count drops to 0 — that case
//        is handled by the tail in @block.
// ============================================================
function reconfigure(ofs) (
  stop_all(ofs);
  tail_active = 0;     // cancel any tail that was in progress

  held_count == 1 ? (
    // ── 1 note: pure pass-through ──────────────────────────
    // Note is forwarded unchanged. It will sustain until
    // the user lifts the key and the tail runs out.
    pass_note  = held_notes[0];
    pass_ch    = held_channels[0];
    master_vel = held_vels[0];
    note_on(ofs, pass_ch, pass_note, held_vels[0]);
  );

  held_count == 2 ? (
    // ── 2 notes: classic trill ─────────────────────────────
    // The average of the two velocities is the target: the corpus shows
    // no systematic accent between the notes of a pair (r10).
    master_vel = (held_vels[0] + held_vels[1]) * 0.5;
    // Equal pitches cannot trill: the pair owns one channel, so the
    // alternation would retrigger the same note (r7). Sustain it.
    held_notes[0] == held_notes[1] ? (
      hold_note(held_notes[0], held_vels[0], held_channels[0], ofs);
    ) : (
      start_trill(held_notes[0], held_notes[1], held_channels[0],
                  0, 0, 0, 1, master_vel, ofs);
    );
  );

  held_count == 3 ? (
    // ── 3 notes: drone + trill ─────────────────────────────
    // Lowest note → sustained drone (pass-through)
    drone_note   = held_notes[0];
    drone_ch     = held_channels[0];
    drone_active = 1;
    note_on(ofs, drone_ch, drone_note, held_vels[0]);

    // Middle and high notes → trill pair
    master_vel = (held_vels[1] + held_vels[2]) * 0.5;
    // Equal pitches: sustain the upper note instead (r7).
    held_notes[1] == held_notes[2] ? (
      hold_note(held_notes[1], held_vels[1], held_channels[1], ofs);
    ) : (
      start_trill(held_notes[1], held_notes[2], held_channels[1],
                  0, 0, 0, 1, master_vel, ofs);
    );
  );

  held_count == 4 ? (
    // ── 4 notes: two parallel trills ───────────────────────
    // Use overall average velocity so both trills have the
    // same rhythmic rate and stay in sync.
    master_vel = (held_vels[0] + held_vels[1] +
                  held_vels[2] + held_vels[3]) * 0.25;

    // A pair whose two pitches are equal is sustained, not trilled (r7).
    held_notes[0] == held_notes[1] ? (
      hold_note(held_notes[0], held_vels[0], held_channels[0], ofs);
    );
    held_notes[2] == held_notes[3] ? (
      hold_note(held_notes[2], held_vels[2], held_channels[2], ofs);
    );

    // Whatever is left is one trill: two voices when both pairs are
    // valid, one voice when only one is (the schedule is shared, r10).
    held_notes[0] != held_notes[1] && held_notes[2] != held_notes[3] ? (
      start_trill(held_notes[0], held_notes[1], held_channels[0],
                  held_notes[2], held_notes[3], held_channels[2],
                  2, master_vel, ofs);
    ) : (
      held_notes[0] != held_notes[1] ? (
        start_trill(held_notes[0], held_notes[1], held_channels[0],
                    0, 0, 0, 1, master_vel, ofs);
      ) : (
        held_notes[2] != held_notes[3] ? (
          start_trill(held_notes[2], held_notes[3], held_channels[2],
                      0, 0, 0, 1, master_vel, ofs);
        );
      );
    );
  );
);


// ============================================================
// FUNCTION: advance_trill(blk)
//
// Walks the schedule through this block, emitting every pending release
// and every attack in ascending offset order. Both voices are always
// emitted inside the same offset, so the stream cannot go backwards
// (r6); the release always lands before the attack that follows it,
// because GATE < 1.
//
// The walk merges two countdowns — the release and the next attack — and
// is written so that it stays correct for any gate, including one that
// would put the release after the attack.
//
//  blk : samples the trill may step through (samplesblock, or the
//        tail-limited advance_len)
// ============================================================
function advance_trill(blk)
  local(pos, e_off, e_on, e)
(
  tr_active ? (

    // Begin where the countdowns were established: the block offset of
    // the attack that set them (r1).
    pos = tr_anchor;
    tr_anchor = 0;

    while (pos < blk) (

      e_off = t1_sounding >= 0 ? tr_off_in : 1000000;
      e_on  = tr_on_in;
      e     = min(e_off, e_on);

      // Strict '>=': an event landing exactly on blk belongs to the next
      // block (offset 0), never to this one (r1).
      pos + e >= blk ? (
        // Nothing else happens in this block: carry both countdowns over
        // as "samples from the block start".
        tr_off_in -= blk - pos;
        tr_on_in  -= blk - pos;
        pos = blk;
      ) : (
        pos += e;

        e_off <= e_on ? (
          // ── release of the sounding note ──
          // The measured articulation: a note ends ~25 ms before the next
          // attack (gate 0.72), which is what makes an alternation read as
          // a trill and not as a smeared legato (r10).
          t1_sounding >= 0 ? note_off(pos, t1_ch, t1_sounding);
          t2_sounding >= 0 ? note_off(pos, t2_ch, t2_sounding);
          t1_sounding = -1;
          t2_sounding = -1;
          tr_off_in = 1000000;
          tr_on_in  = e_on - e;
        ) : (
          // ── attack: both voices at one offset ──
          t1_sounding = t1_phase == 0 ? t1_base : t1_upper;
          note_on(pos, t1_ch, t1_sounding, onset_velocity(master_vel));
          t1_phase = 1 - t1_phase;
          t2_active ? (
            t2_sounding = t2_phase == 0 ? t2_base : t2_upper;
            note_on(pos, t2_ch, t2_sounding, onset_velocity(master_vel));
            t2_phase = 1 - t2_phase;
          );
          tr_count += 1;
          tr_elapsed += tr_period;   // the period that just went by (r12)
          tr_period = next_period();
          tr_off_in = max(64, floor(tr_period * GATE));
          tr_on_in  = tr_period;
        );
      );
    );

  );
);


// ── Initialise timing and ramp defaults ──
ramp_up_s   = floor(slider4 * srate);
ramp_down_s = floor(slider5 * srate);
tail_total  = ramp_down_s;
tr_on_in     = 1000000;   // nothing scheduled until a trill starts
tr_off_in    = 1000000;


// ── SLIDER ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
@slider

// The two ramp windows are durations in samples and need recomputing;
// Rate, Humanize, Balance and Depth are read when the next note is
// generated, so the trill follows them live, mid-alternation (r10/r12).
ramp_up_s   = floor(slider4 * srate);
ramp_down_s = floor(slider5 * srate);
tail_total  = ramp_down_s;


// ── BLOCK ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
@block

// ============================================================
// @block runs once per audio processing block.
// Responsibilities:
//   0. Watch the transport; flush on stop (r4).
//   1. Drain the incoming MIDI queue and update held_notes[].
//   2. Decide how far the engines may advance in this block.
//   3. Advance the trill engines through that range.
//   4. Manage the tail countdown after all notes lift.
// ============================================================

// Recompute the ramp windows in case the sample rate changed (unlikely)
ramp_up_s   = floor(slider4 * srate);
ramp_down_s = floor(slider5 * srate);

// Offset of the most recent event sent in this block. The final
// note-offs may never be sent before it: with Linger at 0 they are
// sent at this offset instead of at 0.
last_ofs = 0;

// ── Step 0: Transport watch (r4) ─────────────────────────────
// Stopping the transport is the one event that can leave a voice
// sounding into the silence: whichever note an engine has on at that
// instant receives its note-off only in a later block, and a stopped
// transport may not run blocks at all. So on the edge out of the
// advancing state, silence everything at offset 0 — before any other
// event of this block, which keeps the offsets ascending.
//
// The held set is kept: the fingers are still on the keys. The engines
// stay parked until either MIDI changes the held set (step 1) or the
// transport advances again; in the second case the voices are restaged
// from the held set, so the trill comes back where it left off.
//
// Locate / loop wrap / project reload is deliberately not handled:
// see the r4 note in the header.
trans_adv_prev = transport_advancing(trans_prev_state);
trans_adv_now  = transport_advancing(play_state);

trans_adv_prev && !trans_adv_now ? (
  // Left the advancing state: cancel any tail, flush, park.
  tail_active  = 0;
  tail_counter = 0;
  stop_all(0);
  trans_parked   = held_count > 0;   // nothing held = nothing to restage
) : (
  !trans_adv_prev && trans_adv_now ? (
    // Advancing again after a stop: restage, unless MIDI already did
    // it in the meantime (step 1 clears trans_parked).
    trans_parked ? reconfigure(0);
    trans_parked = 0;
  );
);

trans_prev_state = play_state;

// ── Step 1: Process incoming MIDI events ──────────────────────
while (midirecv(mpos, msg1, msg2, msg3)) (

  msg_type = msg1 & 0xF0;   // upper nibble: message category
  msg_ch   = msg1 & 0x0F;   // lower nibble: MIDI channel (0–15)

  msg_type == 0x90 && msg3 > 0 ? (
    // ── Note On (velocity > 0) ───────────────────────────────
    // Add to the held list and reconfigure only if the set really
    // changed: a duplicate note-on on a (pitch, channel) pair already
    // held must not restart the engines (audible glitch, ramp reset).
    // The original note-on is NOT forwarded — trill engine(s) or
    // pass-through path inside reconfigure() generate all output
    // note events.
    insert_held(msg2, msg3, msg_ch) ? (
      trans_parked = 0;   // new MIDI restages: nothing is parked any more
      reconfigure(mpos);
    );

  ) : msg_type == 0x80 || (msg_type == 0x90 && msg3 == 0) ? (
    // ── Note Off (explicit 0x80, or note-on with vel=0) ──────
    // Note-on with velocity 0 is a common MIDI shorthand for
    // note-off (running status optimisation from older gear).
    // A note-off that matches no held entry does nothing at all.
    remove_held(msg2, msg_ch) ? (
      trans_parked = 0;

      held_count == 0 ? (
        // Last finger lifted: the tail starts. The trill keeps running
        // and, if Ramp Down is set, slows to a stop across it (r12);
        // Ramp Down 0 stops it at this very note-off.
        tail_active  = 1;
        tail_counter = ramp_down_s;
        tail_total   = ramp_down_s;
      ) : (
        // Notes remain: reconfigure for the new held set.
        reconfigure(mpos);
      );
    );
    // Original note-off is NOT forwarded — trill engine owns note lifecycle.

  ) : (
    // ── All other MIDI (CC, pitch bend, aftertouch, etc.) ────
    // Pass through completely unmodified so the downstream
    // synth still receives expression, mod wheel, sustain, etc.
    last_ofs = mpos;
    midisend(mpos, msg1, msg2, msg3);
  );
);

// ── Step 2: Decide how far the engines may advance ───────────
// Normally the whole block. If the tail expires inside this block, the
// engines must stop exactly at the expiry sample: advancing further would
// emit note-ons after the final note-off, and a note-off at a lower offset
// cannot silence them — the result is a stuck note.
advance_len  = samplesblock;
fence_ofs    = samplesblock - 1;
tail_expired = 0;
tail_active && tail_counter <= samplesblock ? (
  tail_expired = 1;
  advance_len  = max(0, tail_counter);
  fence_ofs    = min(advance_len, samplesblock - 1);
);
// The fence may not precede an event already sent in this block: with
// Ramp Down at 0 the note-off must land on that event, not before it.
fence_ofs = max(fence_ofs, last_ofs);

// ── Step 3: Advance the trill up to advance_len ───────────
// advance_trill() emits attacks and the pending release in ascending
// offset order, both voices at the same offset (r10).
advance_trill(advance_len);

// ── Step 4: Handle the tail countdown ─────────────────────────
tail_active ? (
  tail_counter -= samplesblock;
  tail_expired ? (
    // The tail expired: send note-offs for everything sounding at the
    // fence offset, which is never earlier than the last event sent in
    // this block. The receiving instrument's release envelope provides
    // the audible decay; we just stop scheduling new alternations.
    stop_all(fence_ofs);
    tail_active = 0;
  );
);
